  =============== VMWARE VM PROVISIONING USING ANSIBLE =================
                Technical Implementation & Operations Guide


Automated virtual machine deployment from golden templates with static networking and VMware Distributed Switch support
 
1. Purpose
This guide documents how to provision virtual machines in VMware vSphere using Ansible. The automation clones a prepared golden template, assigns compute and storage resources, configures the virtual network and guest operating-system identity/network settings, and reports deployment results.

2. Scope and deployment patterns
Multi-VM deployment:	Create several VMs in one run (for example Development, UAT and Production)	Loop through a list of VM definitions in vars.yml
Single VM on Distributed Switch:	Deploy one VM to a specified distributed port group	Uses a VM-specific variable set and the dvswitch_name parameter
This guide does not automate creation of datacenters, clusters, datastores, distributed switches, distributed port groups, VLANs, IPAM reservations, firewall rules, DNS records, application installation, or monitoring enrollment. Those items must exist or be handled by separate workflows unless the scope is explicitly extended.

3. Architecture and workflow
Ansible runs from a Linux control node and communicates with vCenter through the VMware API. Guest customization is applied by vSphere to the cloned VM. VMware Tools/open-vm-tools in the guest is required for IP reporting and customization workflows.

Ansible control node	Runs the playbook, Python dependencies and VMware collection
vCenter Server	API endpoint; manages inventory, template cloning, VM hardware and network assignment
Golden template	Prepared base operating system image with VMware Tools/open-vm-tools and supported customization prerequisites
Cluster / ESXi hosts	Compute placement and execution of VM
Datastore	Stores VM configuration and virtual disks
Port group / Distributed port group	Provides VM network connectivity and VLAN policy
Guest OS	Receives hostname, DNS and static IP customization

1.	Operator reviews the request and confirms VM name, IP, resources, target environment and network.
2.	Control node validates inventory, configuration and credentials.
3.	Ansible connects to vCenter and clones the selected template.
4.	VM hardware and disk settings are applied.
5.	Virtual NIC is connected to the requested network.
6.	Guest customization is applied; the VM is powered on.
7.	Automation waits for customization/IP detection where configured.
8.	Operator validates vCenter inventory, guest networking and access.

4. Prerequisites
4.1 Control node
•	Linux control node with supported Python and Ansible versions.
•	Network reachability to vCenter HTTPS/API endpoint (normally TCP 443).
•	DNS resolution and correct time synchronization.
•	Administrative or delegated vCenter account with permissions to clone/create VMs, assign resources, connect NICs and customize guests.
•	Python dependencies compatible with the selected Ansible and community.vmware collection versions.
•	Approved CA certificate available if vCenter uses an internal certificate authority.

4.2 Example installation
Use a Python virtual environment to isolate automation dependencies. Confirm the versions in your environment and pin them for repeatable runs.
# Example on a Debian/Ubuntu-based control node
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y python3 python3-venv python3-pip
 
mkdir -p ~/vmware-automation
cd ~/vmware-automation
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
 
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install pyvmomi requests
 
ansible-galaxy collection install community.vmware
 
ansible --version
ansible-galaxy collection list
python -m pip show pyvmomi requests

Note: Some newer collection versions may require a particular Python/Ansible version or additional dependencies. Check the collection requirements before installation; avoid installing packages into the system Python where the OS manages it.
4.3 VMware prerequisites
•	Target datacenter and cluster exist and are spelled exactly as they appear in vCenter.
•	Target datastore is accessible to the selected cluster/host.
•	VM template exists, is powered off, and is suitable for cloning.
•	Target standard port group or distributed port group exists and is accessible to the target cluster/hosts.
•	IP address, subnet mask, gateway and DNS values have been approved and are not already in use.
•	vCenter account permissions are sufficient. Free ESXi licensing does not support the API write operations required by these modules.
5. Golden template preparation
Prepare and test a template before automation. Template quality directly affects cloning, customization and boot reliability.
5.1 Guest packages and prerequisites
# RHEL / Rocky Linux example
sudo dnf install -y open-vm-tools perl
sudo systemctl enable --now vmtoolsd
 
# Ubuntu example (package names can differ by release)
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y open-vm-tools
sudo systemctl enable --now open-vm-tools

Validate that VMware Tools is running and that the guest OS is supported for vSphere customization. Linux customization generally requires VMware Tools/open-vm-tools and Perl in the template.
5.2 Template hygiene
•	Patch the base OS and remove temporary files, logs and unused packages according to your image standard.
•	Remove machine-specific identity data only using the approved OS-specific template process. Do not blindly remove identity files without understanding the operating-system behavior.
•	Ensure SSH host keys are regenerated on first boot by the template's supported mechanism. If manually deleting host keys, verify that a regeneration service or first-boot process exists.
•	Review cloud-init or other provisioning agents; avoid having two tools compete to configure hostname, IP or DNS.
•	Check firewall, SELinux, SSH, time synchronization and monitoring-agent baseline settings.
•	Power off the template cleanly before converting it to a template.
•	Document template name, OS version, patch date, owner and validation date.
5.3 Convert to template
9.	In vCenter, select the prepared VM.
10.	Confirm it is powered off.
11.	Choose the template conversion action (menu wording varies by vSphere version).
12.	Assign a clear template name and record it in the automation variables.
13.	Test a manual clone and guest customization before using the template in automation.
6. Project layout
vmware-automation/
├── inventory.ini
├── vars.yml
├── secrets.yml              # encrypted with Ansible Vault
├── create_vms.yml           # multi-VM deployment
├── create_single_vm_vds.yml # single VM / distributed switch
