Keep configuration, credentials and playbooks separate. Add version control for playbooks and non-secret variables. Never commit plaintext credentials or Vault passwords.
7. Inventory configuration
# inventory.ini
[local]
localhost ansible_connection=local ansible_python_interpreter=/usr/bin/python3

The VMware API tasks run locally from the control node, so the localhost inventory is used. If a virtual environment is used, set ansible_python_interpreter to the Python executable in that environment (for example, the full path to .venv/bin/python).
8. Credential management with Ansible Vault
Create a secrets file and encrypt it before storing it on disk or in version control.
# Create the file (enter credentials in your editor)
ansible-vault create secrets.yml
 
# Example content inside the encrypted file:
vcenter_user: "automation-account@vsphere.local"
vcenter_pass: "REPLACE_WITH_REAL_SECRET"
 
# Encrypt an existing file:
ansible-vault encrypt secrets.yml
 
# Edit securely:
ansible-vault edit secrets.yml
 
# Verify that it is encrypted:
head -n 1 secrets.yml

•	Use a dedicated least-privilege automation account instead of a personal administrator account.
•	Store the Vault password in an approved secret manager or protected operational process; do not put it in scripts or shell history.
•	Restrict file permissions for the project and encrypted secrets.
•	Rotate credentials periodically and after suspected exposure.
